    {
      "name": "Resilience (manual, optional)",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "Get Hotels - After Simulating Supplier B Down",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Accept",
                "value": "application/json"
              }
            ],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{baseUrl}}/api/hotels?city=delhi",
              "host": [
                "{{baseUrl}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "api",
                "hotels"
              ],
              "query": [
                {
                  "key": "city",
                  "value": "delhi"
                }
              ]
            },
            "description": "Manual test for the assessment's optional 'simulate one supplier being down' scenario. Both mock suppliers live inside the same api container, so there's no single request that takes just one of them down -- reproduce it by restarting the worker with a broken Supplier B URL, then send this request:\n\ndocker compose stop worker\ndocker compose run --rm -e SUPPLIER_B_URL=http://api:3000/does-not-exist worker\n\n(or locally: SUPPLIER_B_URL=http://localhost:1/unreachable npm run worker)\n\nExpected: still 200 OK, with only Supplier A's hotels in the response (Supplier B's activity retries 3 times and is treated as returning zero hotels rather than failing the whole request) -- see getHotelOffersWorkflow.ts's fetchSupplierOrEmpty. Restore the worker afterwards with `docker compose up -d worker` (or a normal `npm run worker`) to undo the override."
          },
          "response": [],
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('Status code is 200 (request succeeds despite one supplier being down)', function () {",
                  "    pm.response.to.have.status(200);",
                  "});",
                  "pm.test('Response is still an array', function () {",
                  "    pm.expect(pm.response.json()).to.be.an('array');",
                  "});"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
